    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 49.606, 405.503, 241.523, 419.778 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-ReguItal",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "4.3. Reduction in the Leakage Current"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 10,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 49.606, 341.845, 545.675, 390.633 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The amount of reduction in the leakage current achieved by utilizing the proposed three registers is evaluated in this section. Four CMOS technology generations, 45 nm, 32 nm, 22 nm, and 16 nm, are considered using a predictive technology model[28,29]."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 11,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 49.606, 272.037, 545.675, 339.061 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The register illustrated in Figure8 is simulated for each technology node where the W/L ratios of the transistors are maintained constant. The leakage current drawn from the power supply is evaluated for the three registers and the results are compared with the leakage current of the original register where only low-Vth transistors are used."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 49.606, 117.513, 545.675, 270.299 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The results are illustrated in Figure9. Note that for the ﬁrst register, the state of the clock signal does not change the results since all of the high-Vth transistors are within the slave latch. For the second and third registers, however, high-Vth transistors exist within the tristate inverters. The state of the clock signal is therefore important in evaluating the results. For example, for the second register, clock signal should be at VSS to guarantee that the initial tristate inverter is not in the high impedance state. Similarly, for the third register, clock signal should be at VDD so that the second tristate inverter located along the feedback path is not in the high impedance state. The leakage current of the original register is therefore compared with the ﬁrst two registers and third register when the clock signal is, respectively, at VSS and VDD."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 49.606, 49.606, 545.675, 115.584 ],
    "font" : "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The leakage current increases with technology, exhibiting a large jump in the 16 nm node. A signiﬁcant amount of reduction in the leakage current, 79% on average, is achieved by the ﬁrst register since the number of high-Vth transistors is higher, as listed in Table2. The second register also achieves a considerable amount of reduction in the leakage current, 13% on average and higher below 32 nm"
  } ]
}